    "summary": "Turn 59 delivered the map handoff for the confirmed codec truncation.\n\nState on entry. Claim C10-command-value-length-survives-the-wire is confirmed in scope: a 70000-byte value is written as 70013 bytes but decoded as 4464 bytes with only 4477 bytes consumed, so the value is shortened and the remaining bytes stay in the reader's stream.\n\nMap update (accepted base version 12). One surface was added for the state value/command codec — disposition infrastructure, with the sources and a reason that states its role as the framing of every command and the confirmed truncation while noting the default 1000-byte command size stays inside the field's range. core_overview gained one open detail recording the same mechanism, the configuration boundary, and the unread reader-side consequence; the map_changes entry covers core_overview with a preserves explanation, and the surface is an independent addition.\n\nFrontier. Ten claims are now settled: seven confirmed violations or consequences (C1 recovery divergence, C2 durable record layout, C3 applied-state divergence, C4 NOOP-commit crash, C6 promoted-leader variant, C7 dead accept-rejection path, C9 recorder row, C10 codec truncation — nine counting the two C9 scenarios) plus two bounded no-violation protections (C5 owner-alive re-proposal, C8 executor timeout enqueue). Two candidates remain paused with their resume conditions, and the only visible administrative item is the scope-issue bookkeeping on the superseded C1 artifact. Undone items recorded rather than absorbed: what a replica reader does with a shifted stream, whether the client caps the command size it builds, and the request-timeout path's real form in a deployment.\n\nNo requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened."
